00:01:59.421 --> 00:02:01.920
And I left out some of the other
things you might feel here.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.870
Because it's not
appropriate on the slide.

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.600
But you all know what
I mean by this, right?

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.240
So this is where
product management

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:12.192
is, in terms of what
you do for any product

00:02:12.192 --> 00:02:13.650
you might be
managing, whether it's

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.260
a technical product, hardware
product, or software product.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:23.392
So if you leave these
definitions out, and step back

00:02:23.392 --> 00:02:24.850
for a minute, and
think about, what

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.170
is it that we're trying to do?

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:33.200
It effectively boils down
to thinking about the user.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.270
And depending on
the product, it is

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:38.660
all about, who is the
actual user of your product?

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.690
And how do you really
focus all your energy?

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:44.360
And when I say
all your energy, I

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.630
mean you and the team
with which you're

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:51.710
working to produce the
product to focus on that user.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:54.790
In some cases, it's
bigger than the user.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:56.780
It is the customer.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:03:00.920
And that applies to certain
segments of products.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.760
And in the case of APIs,
it's not just the customer.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.250
But it's also the developer.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:10.070
And that's what we start with
when we think about product

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:12.230
management for APIs.

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:15.440
So for us, the starting
point, or the constituent,

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.410
who is most important when it
comes to product management

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:20.720
for APIs is the developer.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:25.470
Now as you all know, when it
comes to product management,

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.487
there are three kind of
big aspects out here.

00:03:27.487 --> 00:03:29.820
One is the customer, user,
whatever you want to call it,

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:31.600
depending on your audience.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.360
The second aspect
is the technology.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.670
And the third one
is the business.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:39.250
You, as a product
manager, are trying

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:43.610
to get these three to work in
favor, so that you can drive

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:46.840
growth, drive success,
drive adoption, whatever

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:50.290
the metric you care deeply about
for the success of the mission

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:51.990
you're on.

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.600
But if we really distill
down these three things

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.970
into what exactly you
are trying to achieve

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:59.640
for each one of
these constituents,

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:04.400
it boils down to
delighting the customer,

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.800
driving innovation
with technology,

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.010
and last, but not the least,
driving business impact.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:10.700
That's it.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.710
And you can measure
this 100 different ways

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:14.870
based on your business,
the metrics you can track,

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:17.480
metrics you can't track,
the instrumentation you

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.680
have in the product, or not.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.470
But at the end of the day,
if you don't have a delighted

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:25.880
user or a customer,
and if you're not

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:29.990
able to deliver very good
technical insight and unique

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:33.322
value proposition for your
customers using technology,

00:04:33.322 --> 00:04:35.030
and last but not the
least, if you're not

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:37.520
able to drive actual
impact for your business,

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:39.140
it doesn't matter.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:40.950
As a product
manager, we all fail

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:42.930
if those things don't happen.

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:44.690
And it's kind of on us.

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:46.880
And the kind of
good news/bad news

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:50.010
here is we are kind of
accountable for this.

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:51.510
That's the good news.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:56.490
The bad news is, you may not
have all the responsibility,

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:58.900
or you may not have all
the empowerment you need

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:00.680
or sometimes get through this.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:03.170
And that's what makes all the
product management jobs very

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:06.930
interesting, and challenging
if you're up for it.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:09.480
So when we think
about APIs, how do we

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:13.620
frame this delight,
innovation and impact

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.740
in a different way for APIs?

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:19.841
Well, we start with the notion
of actually the digital value

00:05:19.841 --> 00:05:20.340
chain.

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:24.200
And some of who attended the
previous sessions, either where

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:26.370
we talked about Apigee
products, or otherwise,

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:28.200
have seen this time and again.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:30.220
And the reason we keep
using this framework

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:34.440
is it actually drives home the
point of what we care deeply

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.420
about when it comes to APIs.

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:38.550
And it distills
the point of view

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:42.930
that who the audience or the
user for your products is.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:47.310
If your product is the API,
the user is your developer.

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:51.120
So this evaluation makes that
point very, very clear to you.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:54.540
And the good news here is, when
you're thinking about the API,

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:56.100
depending on where you are--

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.680
either you are providing the
product for the API product

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:00.830
team, or the API
team in this case,

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:02.830
or it's the consuming
developer who

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:07.290
is on the right side
on the screen for you.

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:09.690
That person, or
that user, defines

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:13.920
what is success looking
like for your APIs.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.980
Now whenever I talk about
the API and the developer

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:19.710
being the user, we
also go with the notion

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:23.340
that the APIs are, effectively,
your company's contract

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.590
with the developer.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:27.510
But it expands from there.

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:30.187
Because the API itself can
be used in multiple ways.

00:06:30.187 --> 00:06:31.770
So lets look at the
three typical ways

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:35.490
we think about APIs when
it comes to that contract.

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:39.240
Number one is what we
call API as a service.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.910
And this is very important
in a large organization.

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:43.290
And some of you
probably attended

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:45.500
one of my colleague's
session, [INAUDIBLE],

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:49.190
who talked about the
overall API-first approach

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:51.000
in distributed computing.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.910
This is the premise for a
lot of the modern application

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:57.480
development today,
where APIs the service

00:06:57.480 --> 00:06:58.590
that you're providing.

00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:00.381
And the reason you're
providing that is you

00:07:00.381 --> 00:07:02.520
have developers, who
you're not going to meet,

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:05.560
and who would use your service,
and consume it via the API,

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:07.170
and build something interesting.

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:10.020
And you need to make sure that
they have the most frictionless

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:13.140
way to build things,
and they are only

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:15.090
seeing it as a service.

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:17.130
And for them, it's they
consume that service,

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:18.560
build something, and move on.

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:21.060
And you're going to make sure
that their life is really easy

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:23.191
when it comes to
providing that API,

00:07:23.191 --> 00:07:25.440
making it easy for them to
discover it, making it easy

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:27.090
for them to access it.

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:29.970
And the more people
in the organization

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:33.540
build the APIs as a service,
the overall organization

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:35.760
can move forward in
whatever innovation

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:38.299
they want to do with the
least amount of friction.

00:07:38.299 --> 00:07:39.840
And this is something
we are starting

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:44.490
to see even more now that modern
application development is

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:45.030
happening.

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:47.220
Micro services is taking off.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:49.020
People are doing
interesting workloads

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:51.630
and building containerized
applications.

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:53.940
This becomes even
more important.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:56.760
Because you want this kind
of flourishing innovation

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:00.920
to happen when people are
using API as a service.

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:03.830
Now in this, you can
almost take the view

00:08:03.830 --> 00:08:07.220
that your internal
developers, your own teams,

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:08.660
are the customers.

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:12.800
And this is a kind of slightly
harder shift for a lot

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:14.390
of enterprise, especially.

00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:19.820
Because they are so used to
having one-on-one integration,

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:23.840
or one-on-one agreement within
teams to build something--

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:25.790
which can be, sometimes,
very brittle--

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:28.790
to go from there to something,
which is much more scalable

00:08:28.790 --> 00:08:31.010
when it comes to an
API as a service.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:34.940
But once you have this, then
you are ready for the next step.

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:41.039
And the next step is what we
call as APIs as interaction.

00:08:41.039 --> 00:08:43.760
But show of hands, for
example, how many of you

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:47.360
use YouTube or Netflix
for viewing content?

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:48.740
Most of you, right?

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:50.540
Now the good news
is that you probably

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:54.260
are viewing that content in
different devices all the time,

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:57.510
on the laptop, on your
mobile device, on an Xbox,

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:00.542
on a Chromecast.

00:09:00.542 --> 00:09:02.000
Every very different
device, you're

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:05.900
watching the same content and
interacting with that content.

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:10.310
And we have seen
people use the API

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:12.650
as what we call
Expediency APIs to improve

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:14.550
that part of interaction.

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:17.360
And Netflix, at some point,
really did this well,

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.300
where they would have, actually,
APIs based on the device,

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:24.380
or based on how you're
consuming the actual content,

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:25.915
the experience would change.

00:09:25.915 --> 00:09:28.040
And that doesn't mean that
they going and rewriting

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:32.840
a bunch of applications or
APIs, which are being used

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:34.760
to expose some of the services.

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:36.140
But they actually
change the APIs

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:38.330
on the consumption
side, which is purely

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:40.430
to drive the
differential experience,

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:43.400
and create the right interaction
for you on the right device.

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:47.030
And that's how you're able
to scale to over 300 devices,

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:49.100
for example, in
their case, and still

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:51.350
have the right level
of interaction,

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:54.590
and right level of experience
for you, as a consumer, when

00:09:54.590 --> 00:09:56.650
it comes to that service.

00:09:56.650 --> 00:10:00.650
Now for that to happen, somebody
had to think about the API

00:10:00.650 --> 00:10:03.230
as an experience or
an interaction API.

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:04.970
And that was the product.

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:06.820
And if that product
was not there,

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:08.570
the developer would
not be able to deliver

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:09.944
the interaction
or the experience

00:10:09.944 --> 00:10:13.160
you actually were so
familiar with when you click

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:14.810
Play, and watch something.

00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:17.000
And you felt like,
wow, this is so good.

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:19.370
And you didn't care
about whether the device

00:10:19.370 --> 00:10:21.950
was a tablet, or a
phone, or a laptop.

00:10:21.950 --> 00:10:24.920
And that's what happened when
somebody was able to deliver

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:27.140
APIs as interactions for you.

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:28.820
So that's kind of
this on second level.

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:31.850
The third level is where we take
this one notch up, and actually

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:35.840
really think about
APIs as products.

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:37.970
So the notion of
products immediately

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:40.010
kind of brings a
very specific image

00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:43.610
in your head, which is that
means there is a packaging.

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:45.712
There is something
around pricing.

00:10:45.712 --> 00:10:47.670
And there is something
that on how you actually

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:49.100
deliver this to people.

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:52.340
And these are the aspects
Jon is going to talk later.

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:55.100
But at the core of
this is, effectively,

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:58.280
how do you start to think
about APIs as a product?

00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:00.290
Now if you think about
APIs as a product,

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:03.170
then each one of you who are
playing a product manager

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:05.300
role for whatever
product you're doing,

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:07.160
you have to start
thinking about even

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:09.920
these APIs as your products.

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:12.980
So let's say, for example, one
of you in the audience here has

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:15.590
a software product.

00:11:15.590 --> 00:11:17.502
And I meet a lot of
customers who have,

00:11:17.502 --> 00:11:18.710
actually, a software product.

00:11:18.710 --> 00:11:21.350
And they're trying to build
an ecosystem around it.

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:25.330
So the one set of PMs are really
driving the product itself.

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:27.290
And so how do we
deliver the product,

00:11:27.290 --> 00:11:28.640
and our users can use it?

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:31.580
And then we measure, improve,
and drive the business impact.

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:32.840
Awesome.

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:35.030
There's a set of PMs, then,
who are thinking about,

00:11:35.030 --> 00:11:39.470
how do I take this and deliver
value with my partners?

00:11:39.470 --> 00:11:42.860
And for them to do interesting
integration, and experiences,

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:48.200
and interactions, the only thing
this PM has to offer is an API.

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:49.920
That's the starting point.

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:52.430
So now if I'm an
ecosystem PM, and if I'm

00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:54.830
doing something
interesting with a partner,

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:56.780
and I'm building
some interactions,

00:11:56.780 --> 00:12:00.230
I have to start treating
the API as my product.

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:02.230
What does that mean?

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:05.230
So first and foremost it has
to be a first-class citizen

00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:07.400
in your product portfolio.

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:08.470
So why do I say that?

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:10.920
It cannot be, well,
we have a product.

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:12.930
You were trying to
run a partner program.

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:15.130
Let's figure out, OK, we'll
come up with some API.

00:12:15.130 --> 00:12:19.690
No, you've got to treat that
API as a first-class product,

00:12:19.690 --> 00:12:22.300
as if, if your
systems went down,

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:24.130
and a customer is
going to scream,

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:26.880
you have to apply the same
rigor when the API goes down,

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:28.630
and your partner is
going to actually fail

00:12:28.630 --> 00:12:30.190
in delivering something.

00:12:30.190 --> 00:12:33.250
So you have to treat it
with that level of care.

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:35.780
But before even you get
to delivering that API

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:39.190
with that level of care, you
have to start with the notion

00:12:39.190 --> 00:12:41.950
that, we need a great API.

00:12:41.950 --> 00:12:44.140
And if that API has
to be great, what are

00:12:44.140 --> 00:12:45.700
the characteristics for that?

00:12:45.700 --> 00:12:47.920
Why should the
developer use that API?

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:50.350
What is unique about that API?

00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:53.770
How would I go and make
this API really frictionless

00:12:53.770 --> 00:12:54.487
for a developer?

00:12:54.487 --> 00:12:57.070
Those are the kind of things you
got to think about before you

00:12:57.070 --> 00:12:58.887
actually have any API.

00:12:58.887 --> 00:13:00.220
Now here's an interesting piece.

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:03.400
For all the good software
products out there,

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:05.320
hopefully there is
an API already used

00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:06.439
to build that product.

00:13:06.439 --> 00:13:08.230
And then the question
is, how do you really

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:10.300
make that available
to somebody outside

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:13.540
for certain functionality, or
certain aspects of the product,

00:13:13.540 --> 00:13:15.130
which can be used
for integration

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:17.470
or doing some ecosystem work?

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:19.260
But if you don't
have that API, you've

00:13:19.260 --> 00:13:22.720
got to go back, and figure out,
how do you create that API?

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:24.610
But thinking about it
more like a product,

00:13:24.610 --> 00:13:26.280
as in you're
looking at the user,

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:28.210
you're thinking about
their requirements.

00:13:28.210 --> 00:13:29.080
What do they need?

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:32.080
What is the service level
you need to provide for them?

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:34.930
And then, more importantly,
how do I deliver this to them

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:36.200
in a frictionless manner?

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:38.650
So that's kind of the starting
point of creating that API

00:13:38.650 --> 00:13:41.230
and delivering it
to your developers.

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:45.819
The second one is, once you
have that API, it is not done.

00:13:45.819 --> 00:13:48.360
Because like any other software
product, or any other product

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:51.640
you product manage,
you're constantly learning

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:53.200
and you're improving.

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:54.700
That means you're
looking at data.

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:56.550
You're looking at feedback.

00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:59.290
And you're figuring out,
where are the bottlenecks?

00:13:59.290 --> 00:14:00.970
Where could we do
interesting things

00:14:00.970 --> 00:14:02.680
with this particular API?

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:06.220
And what can I do better,
so that my partner,

00:14:06.220 --> 00:14:11.050
or my ecosystem folks,
can do something

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:13.450
more interesting going
forward, and innovate

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:15.190
on top of my product?

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:17.950
So that comes to really
thinking about instrumentation,

00:14:17.950 --> 00:14:19.820
measurement, and
refinement as you

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:22.249
go with the release of the API.

00:14:22.249 --> 00:14:23.290
So this is what we call--

00:14:23.290 --> 00:14:24.910
it's not the launch of the API.

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:27.310
It is almost thinking about
the lifecycle of the API,

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:29.680
so that you're measuring,
looking at the data,

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:31.390
and improving what
needs to happen

00:14:31.390 --> 00:14:33.440
with that particular API.

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:35.900
The third one is, of
course, the impact piece,

00:14:35.900 --> 00:14:37.550
which is that it's all great.

00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:38.540
You have an API.

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:40.940
You have a good way
to learn and improve.

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:43.220
You're probably on to
version 3 of the API.

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:46.850
So you went from kind of a
beta program, to a proper GA,

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:48.880
and now you're in
version 3, which

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:51.870
should be the most mature way
for people to actually access

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:55.430
your systems of software, and
build something interesting.

00:14:55.430 --> 00:14:58.250
But the third one is impact.

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:00.830
Is that particular
API really driving

00:15:00.830 --> 00:15:04.070
any value and impact for
you, and your customers,

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:04.836
more importantly?

00:15:04.836 --> 00:15:06.710
Because remember, if
you have a product which

00:15:06.710 --> 00:15:09.015
has both the software
product and an API,

00:15:09.015 --> 00:15:10.640
you're looking at
two sides of the coin

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:12.080
when it comes to a business.

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:14.210
You can keep selling
more software,

00:15:14.210 --> 00:15:17.150
and/or you can also drive
information and experience

00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:18.680
through your APIs.

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:22.070
And that can actually accrue
benefit back into the product.

00:15:22.070 --> 00:15:23.870
Or both can go independently.

00:15:23.870 --> 00:15:26.480
And they suddenly become
two streams of businesses.

00:15:26.480 --> 00:15:28.190
These all depend on
what you're doing,

00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:32.750
and what area, or what domain
you are in, and how it happens.

00:15:32.750 --> 00:15:36.530
But the end of the day, that
has to be an impact measurement.

00:15:36.530 --> 00:15:39.530
And this impact measurement
can be sometimes very fuzzy.

00:15:39.530 --> 00:15:41.870
Because you can start
with the premise

00:15:41.870 --> 00:15:44.210
that I'm going to
generate a lot of revenue

00:15:44.210 --> 00:15:46.760
from this particular API.

00:15:46.760 --> 00:15:49.130
So I have customers who
always come and say,

00:15:49.130 --> 00:15:50.720
we have a core business.

00:15:50.720 --> 00:15:51.860
That's growing very well.

00:15:51.860 --> 00:15:52.930
We're happy with that.

00:15:52.930 --> 00:15:54.680
We want to do this API
program, because we

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:57.980
want to unlock a whole
new set of revenue.

00:15:57.980 --> 00:15:59.820
That's great.

00:15:59.820 --> 00:16:02.080
But the challenge is
the API itself does not

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:03.250
generate revenue.

00:16:03.250 --> 00:16:06.170
You've got to invest in it,
like you do for any other go

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:06.670
to market.

00:16:06.670 --> 00:16:09.280
Because now, you have
API as a product.

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:11.172
How are you going to
take it to market?

00:16:11.172 --> 00:16:12.130
Who are your customers?

00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:14.470
How are you going to make sure
there's a demand for that?

00:16:14.470 --> 00:16:16.886
How are you going to make sure
there's awareness for that?

00:16:16.886 --> 00:16:18.360
How are you going
to convert that?

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:19.776
How are you going
to then monetize

00:16:19.776 --> 00:16:22.187
that API to drive revenue
if that's the goal you have?

00:16:22.187 --> 00:16:24.520
So that's one of the things
you've got to think through,

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:27.130
is when you think about impact,
you're also thinking about,

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:30.910
how do I take this API to
market to drive that impact?

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:35.710
The second notion is that you
have no revenue attachment

00:16:35.710 --> 00:16:37.810
whatsoever to this API.

00:16:37.810 --> 00:16:42.310
But it's a fantastic way to
build loyalty and stickiness

00:16:42.310 --> 00:16:44.590
to the platform.

00:16:44.590 --> 00:16:46.420
And we've seen
examples of this over

00:16:46.420 --> 00:16:50.760
and over again, whether you take
a very simple example like what

00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:54.190
Slack does today, or
Google Maps API does today,

00:16:54.190 --> 00:16:57.550
is that the API itself may or
may not generate the revenue.

00:16:57.550 --> 00:16:59.290
But the integration
it allows you to do,

00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:03.730
the experience it creates, makes
people keep using the product.

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:05.137
And that is gold.

00:17:05.137 --> 00:17:06.970
Because at the end of
the day, what you want

00:17:06.970 --> 00:17:09.310
is people to continue to
use your core product.

00:17:09.310 --> 00:17:13.560
And the API has become a way to
kind of find out, if you may,

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:16.210
and bring in more audiences,
and bring in more partners who

00:17:16.210 --> 00:17:17.990
can build stuff on it.

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:20.470
And when you really get to the
scale where a lot of people

00:17:20.470 --> 00:17:23.035
are building on top
of your product,

00:17:23.035 --> 00:17:24.910
and that's when you can
really start to think

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:27.470
about yourself as a platform.

00:17:27.470 --> 00:17:29.300
And that gets
really interesting.

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:31.910
And then you really don't
care about monetizing

00:17:31.910 --> 00:17:32.864
one particular aspect.

00:17:32.864 --> 00:17:35.030
Because now you have,
suddenly, a lot of opportunity

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:38.799
to think about monetization
in a very different way.

00:17:38.799 --> 00:17:41.090
So the core of this, when
you think about API products,

00:17:41.090 --> 00:17:43.340
it goes back to
those three things.

00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:47.124
Number one, you've got to
treat this as a product.

00:17:47.124 --> 00:17:49.040
And that means you've
got to pay attention to,

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:51.800
what are you delivering in
the unique value proposition

00:17:51.800 --> 00:17:55.670
to a customer, or a user of
that particular product, which

00:17:55.670 --> 00:17:57.350
is your API?

00:17:57.350 --> 00:17:59.750
Number two, you got to
think about how you're

00:17:59.750 --> 00:18:02.390
going to constantly
learn, look at the data,

00:18:02.390 --> 00:18:04.984
keep improving, and then
waiting on top of that.

00:18:04.984 --> 00:18:07.400
So you don't stop as soon as
you release the first version

00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:09.170
of the API, just
like you wouldn't

00:18:09.170 --> 00:18:11.690
stop releasing your first
version of software,

00:18:11.690 --> 00:18:14.120
or one model of a car, or
one model of a [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:14.120 --> 00:18:18.810
good product, or one particular
SKU [INAUDIBLE] or anything

00:18:18.810 --> 00:18:19.310
else.

00:18:19.310 --> 00:18:21.143
You're going to constantly
look at the data,

00:18:21.143 --> 00:18:23.900
and improve, and move
forward from there.

00:18:23.900 --> 00:18:25.680
And the last one is
really think about it

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:29.780
in terms of, what is the real
impact measurement out here?

00:18:29.780 --> 00:18:31.040
Are you measuring revenue?

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:34.580
Are you measuring the value
back to your core product?

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:36.770
And how do you
nurture that, and not

00:18:36.770 --> 00:18:39.740
imagine that just because
you have an API, suddenly,

00:18:39.740 --> 00:18:41.570
things are going to
magically appear?

00:18:41.570 --> 00:18:44.360
It takes a lot of effort to
take that API, as a product,

00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:47.420
to the market, just like
you spent a lot of energy

00:18:47.420 --> 00:18:49.370
taking something
else to the market,

00:18:49.370 --> 00:18:51.089
in terms of your product.

00:18:51.089 --> 00:18:53.630
So when you think about those
aspects, that's where we really

00:18:53.630 --> 00:18:56.570
think about, we have
to treat the API

00:18:56.570 --> 00:18:59.660
as a product in this new
economy, our digital economy,

00:18:59.660 --> 00:19:01.070
if I can say that.

00:19:01.070 --> 00:19:03.354
And that's where a lot of
you, as a product manager,

00:19:03.354 --> 00:19:05.270
especially if you're
looking at anything which

00:19:05.270 --> 00:19:07.360
has remotely to do
with technology,

00:19:07.360 --> 00:19:10.280
you've got to think
about having an API.

00:19:10.280 --> 00:19:13.410
You also have to think about
even other products which

00:19:13.410 --> 00:19:14.540
can have APIs.

00:19:14.540 --> 00:19:17.360
I mean, we have
customers who do anything

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:24.410
from financial transaction,
to logistics, to data,

00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:27.170
to sometimes even IOT devices.

00:19:27.170 --> 00:19:29.240
All of these things have APIs.

00:19:29.240 --> 00:19:31.790
And they're trying to
build something around it.

00:19:31.790 --> 00:19:34.310
And API has become
a way, or means,

00:19:34.310 --> 00:19:37.619
to deliver that value
differently to different users.

00:19:37.619 --> 00:19:39.410
And some of them do it
in a platform sense.

00:19:39.410 --> 00:19:41.300
Some of them do it
in the product sense.

00:19:41.300 --> 00:19:45.200
Some of them do it interaction,
or in terms of services.

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:48.590
So hopefully, I've given you
a very basic but important

00:19:48.590 --> 00:19:50.600
framework to think
through how you

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:54.080
can go from API as a service,
to API for interactions,

00:19:54.080 --> 00:19:55.190
to API as a product.

00:19:55.190 --> 00:19:59.360
And what are some of the key
principles around building APIs

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:00.560
as products?

00:20:00.560 --> 00:20:02.120
Now that's a framework.

00:20:02.120 --> 00:20:04.700
That's a good theoretical
framework for all of you

00:20:04.700 --> 00:20:06.300
to have in your head.

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:08.420
But it's always fun
when you have somebody

00:20:08.420 --> 00:20:10.430
who is practicing
this, and they can

00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:12.260
share what they have
learned, and how they

00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:14.360
think about this in real life.

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:17.030
So to do that, I'm
going to welcome Jon.

00:20:17.030 --> 00:20:20.630
Jon Spinney heads up API
products for Pitney Bowes.

00:20:20.630 --> 00:20:22.880
And he's going to come and
share some of his learning.

00:20:22.880 --> 00:20:25.700
Please welcome Jon.

00:20:25.700 --> 00:20:28.710
JON SPINNEY: Thanks,
appreciate it.

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:32.902
That was fantastic, in terms
of those three pillars.

00:20:32.902 --> 00:20:35.500
So I'm Jon Spinney
from Pitney Bowes.

00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:41.450
A little bit about us, we're
almost 100-year-old company.

00:20:41.450 --> 00:20:47.450
And we work across a variety
of different lines of business.

00:20:47.450 --> 00:20:49.700
We do identity management,
location, things like that.

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:53.120
We process a lot of
transactions on a daily basis,

00:20:53.120 --> 00:20:59.700
billions across what we call a
borderless world of commerce.

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:03.510
You know, I sometimes
think about the role a lot,

00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:05.970
and spend an inordinate amount
of time thinking about it.

00:21:05.970 --> 00:21:10.910
And an API in and of itself
is, I kind think of it

00:21:10.910 --> 00:21:13.530
as like an access
method to things

00:21:13.530 --> 00:21:16.627
that we have, and have
had for a long time.

00:21:16.627 --> 00:21:18.960
And one of the comments that's
really resonated with me,

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:20.670
especially at this
conference, has

00:21:20.670 --> 00:21:24.810
been the transition
from we're in the midst

00:21:24.810 --> 00:21:26.880
of lifting and shifting.

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:29.190
We've had customers
for a long time that

00:21:29.190 --> 00:21:31.530
have been using
products, that have

00:21:31.530 --> 00:21:35.100
had APIs available from them.

00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:37.290
And they can latch
onto those things.

00:21:37.290 --> 00:21:39.810
But we started a digital
transformation journey

00:21:39.810 --> 00:21:43.350
a couple of years
ago where we wanted

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:47.670
to introduce APIs as products
available as a service.

00:21:47.670 --> 00:21:50.280
And that was a new
thing for the company.

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:53.250
And we encountered all
of the typical challenges

00:21:53.250 --> 00:21:57.060
that someone going through a
digital transformation shift

00:21:57.060 --> 00:21:58.500
goes through.

00:21:58.500 --> 00:22:01.440
And I, sometimes, think
of introducing these kinds

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:03.630
of products as the other 80%.

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:06.870
Once you have the API, what is
all the other 80% of the things

00:22:06.870 --> 00:22:09.880
that you have to do in order to
operationalize it and turn it

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:12.230
into a product?

00:22:12.230 --> 00:22:16.930
And I'll talk about some of
those things here in a second.

00:22:16.930 --> 00:22:18.780
So when we started
looking at how

00:22:18.780 --> 00:22:22.950
we would do this to help an
existing incumbent customer

00:22:22.950 --> 00:22:27.060
base, as well as go out and find
net new customers to consume

00:22:27.060 --> 00:22:29.040
APIs.

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:32.970
To Bala's point, we put people
first, customers, users.

00:22:32.970 --> 00:22:35.140
But by people I
mean, ultimately,

00:22:35.140 --> 00:22:38.490
the people who are going
to, either use these APIs,

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:42.090
or their partners in
business or finance

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:45.270
who are helping them purchase
and bring in these APIs

00:22:45.270 --> 00:22:46.980
into their organization.

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:49.860
But we've had folks
in telecommunications,

00:22:49.860 --> 00:22:51.540
and insurance, and
financial services,

00:22:51.540 --> 00:22:53.280
and other industries
use our software

00:22:53.280 --> 00:22:55.800
that's been on premise for
years, and years, and years.

00:22:55.800 --> 00:22:57.370
And there have been APIs
available from that.

00:22:57.370 --> 00:22:58.286
But what we've found--

00:22:58.286 --> 00:23:01.230
I'll give you a real example.

00:23:01.230 --> 00:23:06.570
A customer that we've been doing
business with for a few decades

00:23:06.570 --> 00:23:10.320
has been using our stuff on
premise that entire time.

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:14.220
And there was a new product
team inside that company--

00:23:14.220 --> 00:23:17.310
and they specialize
in home security--

00:23:17.310 --> 00:23:21.330
that discovered our
self-service APIs on their own

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:23.880
through a paid search
campaign that we ran.

00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:28.950
And that team had no
idea that their company

00:23:28.950 --> 00:23:32.280
had been doing business with
us for that amount of time.

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:35.250
They discovered a new
way to consume things

00:23:35.250 --> 00:23:38.010
that we've provided
for a number of years,

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:40.440
but in the delivery vehicle
or consumption pattern

00:23:40.440 --> 00:23:43.010
that they're used to.

00:23:43.010 --> 00:23:44.350
And that was a recent discovery.

00:23:44.350 --> 00:23:47.304
But when we first
started our journey

00:23:47.304 --> 00:23:48.720
towards digital
transformation, we

00:23:48.720 --> 00:23:52.030
had some hypotheses
about what people needed.

00:23:52.030 --> 00:23:54.150
We've since learned about that.

00:23:54.150 --> 00:23:57.210
But originally, what
we discovered was,

00:23:57.210 --> 00:24:01.860
let's focus on the people
first, and what their needs are,

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:07.087
which can help us determine
how we price things, which then

00:24:07.087 --> 00:24:09.420
informs how we build our
product, which then informs how

00:24:09.420 --> 00:24:10.795
we build our back
office, and all

00:24:10.795 --> 00:24:13.560
the supporting
infrastructure to support it.

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:20.279
It seems almost irrational
sometimes as a product person--

00:24:20.279 --> 00:24:22.320
as product people, we have
a tendency, sometimes,

00:24:22.320 --> 00:24:24.450
to put our products
before other things.

00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:27.330
Because we get so excited
about our own products.

00:24:27.330 --> 00:24:30.960
But if we apply a discipline
where we put people first,

00:24:30.960 --> 00:24:32.070
price can then follow.

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:34.050
Product can follow
thereafter, and then

00:24:34.050 --> 00:24:38.054
the back office infrastructure
to support your product--

00:24:38.054 --> 00:24:39.720
all of the things
that you need to have,

00:24:39.720 --> 00:24:42.750
in terms of operations, and
sales, and lead generation,

00:24:42.750 --> 00:24:45.300
and fulfillment, and
provisioning, and activation,

00:24:45.300 --> 00:24:48.790
and billing, and the other 80%.

00:24:48.790 --> 00:24:53.820
It starts to define itself for
you, which is a magical thing.

00:24:53.820 --> 00:24:57.360
And we certainly didn't
stumble upon it that way.

00:24:57.360 --> 00:25:00.060
So when we started
with people, we

00:25:00.060 --> 00:25:04.080
went out and talked to our
existing customer base.

00:25:04.080 --> 00:25:09.240
And what we had found was
two types of classifications

00:25:09.240 --> 00:25:10.530
or archetypes.

00:25:10.530 --> 00:25:12.720
One was a commercial developer.

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:15.459
And the other was a
corporate developer.

00:25:15.459 --> 00:25:17.250
And we came up with
those terms on our own.

00:25:17.250 --> 00:25:19.499
But basically, when we looked
at all of our customers,

00:25:19.499 --> 00:25:22.980
we found some customers
that were using our products

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:26.190
on premise for a long time to
build other products that they

00:25:26.190 --> 00:25:28.080
sold.

00:25:28.080 --> 00:25:30.690
And then we also found
types of customers that

00:25:30.690 --> 00:25:31.900
were more corporate nature.

00:25:31.900 --> 00:25:34.530
They were using our
products on premise

00:25:34.530 --> 00:25:38.520
to solve an internal business
problem or a workflow

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:39.610
to gain some efficiencies.

00:25:39.610 --> 00:25:41.980
So one was sort of on the
side of generating revenue.

00:25:41.980 --> 00:25:45.210
The other was on the
side of saving money.

00:25:45.210 --> 00:25:47.730
In reality, what
we found, though,

00:25:47.730 --> 00:25:52.290
is that we're now talking
to a lot of product managers

00:25:52.290 --> 00:25:53.260
on the commercial side.

00:25:53.260 --> 00:25:55.343
So we're having product
manager to product manager

00:25:55.343 --> 00:25:58.085
conversations with companies
that want to consume APIs

00:25:58.085 --> 00:26:00.880
where you have product
people doing assessments,

00:26:00.880 --> 00:26:03.700
and evaluations, and working
with their developers

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:06.580
at the same time to decide
what API makes sense,

00:26:06.580 --> 00:26:08.900
in order to augment or
enhance their product.

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:11.470
So we've landed on these
types of personas, these two

00:26:11.470 --> 00:26:14.080
users or customers, that
we now see a pattern

00:26:14.080 --> 00:26:16.690
for in the two-year
journey that we've

00:26:16.690 --> 00:26:20.450
been on through
digital transformation.

00:26:20.450 --> 00:26:23.320
And so Janet is the--

00:26:23.320 --> 00:26:25.090
we used to call a
commercial developer.

00:26:25.090 --> 00:26:26.980
And we now have
learned that we're

00:26:26.980 --> 00:26:29.830
working more with--
rather than developers,

00:26:29.830 --> 00:26:32.830
we're working with product
managers, where developers

00:26:32.830 --> 00:26:34.030
are testing things out.

00:26:34.030 --> 00:26:36.520
But then we have financial
buyers and the product

00:26:36.520 --> 00:26:38.950
management organization.

00:26:38.950 --> 00:26:40.630
And again, her
interests are she's

00:26:40.630 --> 00:26:43.930
seeking a service to augment or
enhance her existing product,

00:26:43.930 --> 00:26:46.540
or a solution that she
sells in the marketplace.

00:26:46.540 --> 00:26:49.630
Whereas Dario, what
we used to classify

00:26:49.630 --> 00:26:51.430
as kind of a
corporate developer,

00:26:51.430 --> 00:26:53.050
we found is now
more like a business

00:26:53.050 --> 00:26:56.117
process-oriented kind of person
inside a large corporation.

00:26:56.117 --> 00:26:58.450
It can work in any department
across that organization--

00:26:58.450 --> 00:27:01.139
so like telecommunications
provider, for example.

00:27:01.139 --> 00:27:02.680
And there's a real
estate department.

00:27:02.680 --> 00:27:03.850
There's an engineering
department.

00:27:03.850 --> 00:27:05.266
There is an
operations department.

00:27:05.266 --> 00:27:06.760
And there is a
retail department,

00:27:06.760 --> 00:27:08.050
so forth, and so forth.

00:27:08.050 --> 00:27:09.894
We've found lots
of folks like that

00:27:09.894 --> 00:27:13.510
that have business process and
workflow problems that they

00:27:13.510 --> 00:27:15.280
need to solve.

00:27:15.280 --> 00:27:19.340
Where our APIs, as an ingredient
to that overall workflow

00:27:19.340 --> 00:27:21.174
and business process,
is helping them

00:27:21.174 --> 00:27:23.590
achieve the efficiencies and
gains that they want to find,

00:27:23.590 --> 00:27:25.881
and making them more streamlined
in their organization.

00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:36.180
So Janet and Dario, when we
set out on this journey--

00:27:36.180 --> 00:27:40.170
in talking to them,
we found out that they

00:27:40.170 --> 00:27:43.747
had some common goals,
despite their differences

00:27:43.747 --> 00:27:46.080
and objectives, in terms of
generating revenue or saving

00:27:46.080 --> 00:27:47.710
money.

00:27:47.710 --> 00:27:52.872
And those common goals,
they're performance-based,

00:27:52.872 --> 00:27:54.830
whatever they were,
essentially, measured upon,

00:27:54.830 --> 00:27:58.070
in terms of their own
individual performance.

00:27:58.070 --> 00:27:59.370
Those were different.

00:27:59.370 --> 00:28:02.630
However, there must-haves
and there must-not-have

00:28:02.630 --> 00:28:03.800
were identical.

00:28:03.800 --> 00:28:05.300
It was really kind
of interesting.

00:28:05.300 --> 00:28:06.950
And we were sampling
the community,

00:28:06.950 --> 00:28:09.270
in order to find these.

00:28:09.270 --> 00:28:14.060
And some of the must-haves
are kind of obvious things.

00:28:14.060 --> 00:28:18.770
Simple, easy, self-service
learn experiences,

00:28:18.770 --> 00:28:25.430
well-documented documentation,
simple checkout flows,

00:28:25.430 --> 00:28:30.230
and learning, and buying,
these kinds of things,

00:28:30.230 --> 00:28:31.400
they both shared these.

00:28:31.400 --> 00:28:39.140
They both share a desire to
have a frictionless self-service

00:28:39.140 --> 00:28:40.790
kind of experience
where they can learn

00:28:40.790 --> 00:28:43.790
about the product themselves.

00:28:43.790 --> 00:28:48.620
They could buy the product
with little interaction--

00:28:48.620 --> 00:28:50.870
or at least low friction--

00:28:50.870 --> 00:28:53.300
get their access keys,
and their secrets,

00:28:53.300 --> 00:28:57.050
get started, use the
product, buy the product,

00:28:57.050 --> 00:29:00.470
and get support for the product
all in one central destination.

00:29:00.470 --> 00:29:05.830
And those common goals were what
we set out to go and achieve.

00:29:05.830 --> 00:29:08.400
So we made a
promise to ourselves

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:10.140
and to all of our
customers that was what

00:29:10.140 --> 00:29:12.810
we were going to go and do.

00:29:12.810 --> 00:29:16.530
And it seems simple
enough on the surface.

00:29:16.530 --> 00:29:20.040
But actually, there's a
lot of stuff in there.

00:29:20.040 --> 00:29:23.160
How they learn
about it required us

00:29:23.160 --> 00:29:25.890
to work across multiple
different teams

00:29:25.890 --> 00:29:30.120
inside our company to market
appropriately and message

00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:31.740
the right way.

00:29:31.740 --> 00:29:35.700
How they bought it, we were
forced to adjust our billing

00:29:35.700 --> 00:29:38.220
practices.

00:29:38.220 --> 00:29:42.180
How they got it forced us to
change how we communicated.

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:44.580
We had to send automated
notifications and emails

00:29:44.580 --> 00:29:45.570
about where they go.

00:29:45.570 --> 00:29:46.750
And then where do
they go from there?

00:29:46.750 --> 00:29:48.600
And we had all of these
back-office systems

00:29:48.600 --> 00:29:50.940
that each did those silos.

00:29:50.940 --> 00:29:52.861
And we had to bring
all those together.

00:29:52.861 --> 00:29:55.110
So as an organization, it
was very challenging for us.

00:29:55.110 --> 00:29:58.860
But we knew we needed
to deliver this promise.

00:29:58.860 --> 00:30:02.260
And then the things around usage
and payments were also really,

00:30:02.260 --> 00:30:05.470
really were challenging.

00:30:05.470 --> 00:30:07.425
One of the key things
we found was that--

00:30:11.640 --> 00:30:14.610
over the years, we were working
with our incumbent customers.

00:30:14.610 --> 00:30:17.460
They said, we love
your products.

00:30:17.460 --> 00:30:20.430
But we really don't
like your process.

00:30:20.430 --> 00:30:22.937
It takes a really long time for
us to get to use your stuff.

00:30:22.937 --> 00:30:25.020
We've got to go through
this difficult contracting

00:30:25.020 --> 00:30:29.460
thing, where our lawyers are
going back and forth on things,

00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:34.320
where we have to set up and
create custom provisions.

00:30:34.320 --> 00:30:37.449
And your bills, I get
three different or four

00:30:37.449 --> 00:30:38.490
different bills from you.

00:30:38.490 --> 00:30:40.680
And I'm not sure which
product it's for.

00:30:40.680 --> 00:30:43.390
Because we have so many
products inside the company.

00:30:43.390 --> 00:30:45.390
So one of the key things
we set out to go and do

00:30:45.390 --> 00:30:48.540
is deliver unified
contracting and billing.

00:30:48.540 --> 00:30:51.810
And that enabled us to
actually deliver this promise.

00:30:51.810 --> 00:30:53.970
Because the rest of it
sort of fell into place

00:30:53.970 --> 00:30:57.960
around the marketing and learn
aspects, buying, and getting,

00:30:57.960 --> 00:31:02.010
using, and paying, and so forth.

00:31:02.010 --> 00:31:04.710
But before we got
there, one of the key

00:31:04.710 --> 00:31:08.340
challenges that we had was,
not only the complexity

00:31:08.340 --> 00:31:11.490
of the number of products
that we had, but also

00:31:11.490 --> 00:31:15.930
the variety and the diversity,
and the variable value

00:31:15.930 --> 00:31:16.960
associated with that.

00:31:16.960 --> 00:31:20.670
So pretty simple to say to
yourself, OK, I'm going to--

00:31:20.670 --> 00:31:22.440
the customer says they
only want one bill

00:31:22.440 --> 00:31:25.640
from me and one contract.

00:31:25.640 --> 00:31:28.050
But how do I do that
for a myriad of products

00:31:28.050 --> 00:31:30.930
that all have different
value, maybe different usage

00:31:30.930 --> 00:31:33.014
restrictions,
things like that you

00:31:33.014 --> 00:31:36.220
know that we have to think about
from a business perspective?

00:31:36.220 --> 00:31:39.540
So as an example
of that complexity,

00:31:39.540 --> 00:31:44.250
one of our lines of business
is location intelligence.

00:31:44.250 --> 00:31:47.280
And within that, we've
been assembling data sets

00:31:47.280 --> 00:31:48.810
for a number of years.

00:31:48.810 --> 00:31:52.010
And we've got a
tremendous amount of data.

00:31:52.010 --> 00:31:57.220
Each one of these data
sets is different.

00:31:57.220 --> 00:31:59.470
It has a distinctive value.

00:31:59.470 --> 00:32:01.410
It has a distinctive price.

00:32:01.410 --> 00:32:04.620
And if you opened up like our
old-school on-premise beige

00:32:04.620 --> 00:32:08.195
book or price book, it
would be 800 pages long.

00:32:08.195 --> 00:32:11.070
And we can't give that
to a customer that's

00:32:11.070 --> 00:32:13.950
expecting the frictionless
kind of self-service experience

00:32:13.950 --> 00:32:17.440
that they'd want from
a modern API product.

00:32:17.440 --> 00:32:21.990
So that was one complexity
around the breadth that we had.

00:32:21.990 --> 00:32:23.910
And we needed to abstract that.

00:32:23.910 --> 00:32:26.220
The other was around variety.

00:32:26.220 --> 00:32:28.620
We've got all these
different types of data.

00:32:28.620 --> 00:32:33.390
And describing the value
has always been a challenge.

00:32:33.390 --> 00:32:37.860
But as we moved to more of a
self-service delivery model,

00:32:37.860 --> 00:32:43.080
it became increasingly
painful for us

00:32:43.080 --> 00:32:46.230
to learn that we needed
to find a way, really,

00:32:46.230 --> 00:32:48.090
to abstract away
this complexity,

00:32:48.090 --> 00:32:50.310
and deliver on our promise
of self-service simplicity

00:32:50.310 --> 00:32:53.640
across all stages of the journey
that the customer was going

00:32:53.640 --> 00:32:54.600
to go through.

00:32:54.600 --> 00:32:56.730
And then at the
same time, there was

00:32:56.730 --> 00:32:58.890
some additional
complications or complexity

00:32:58.890 --> 00:33:01.260
added in around the
verticals that we serve.

00:33:01.260 --> 00:33:03.360
So there was very
vertical-specific

00:33:03.360 --> 00:33:06.370
messaging that we needed
to do, and so forth.

00:33:06.370 --> 00:33:09.690
So as we started
thinking about this,

00:33:09.690 --> 00:33:13.740
and we started shopping
around for partners to help,

00:33:13.740 --> 00:33:15.990
it took a while.

00:33:15.990 --> 00:33:19.500
But we went from
a messy situation,

00:33:19.500 --> 00:33:24.540
where we had a lot of different
data, with different values,

00:33:24.540 --> 00:33:27.120
with different messages that
seemed overwhelming from

00:33:27.120 --> 00:33:30.284
a product manager standpoint
in order to position,

00:33:30.284 --> 00:33:32.700
and market effectively, and
help people understand without

00:33:32.700 --> 00:33:34.241
lengthy conversations,
and so forth--

00:33:34.241 --> 00:33:38.800
some of the must-not-haves
that Dario and Janet shared--

00:33:38.800 --> 00:33:44.467
to move to more of a model that
made sense that abstracted away

00:33:44.467 --> 00:33:45.300
all that complexity.

00:33:45.300 --> 00:33:46.630
So we looked at two things.

00:33:46.630 --> 00:33:49.524
We looked at the
Chinese menu of all

00:33:49.524 --> 00:33:50.940
of our products
in that beige book

00:33:50.940 --> 00:33:52.314
that I mentioned,
where you could

00:33:52.314 --> 00:33:55.620
have dozens of
categories with dozens

00:33:55.620 --> 00:33:58.860
of prices within each category.

00:33:58.860 --> 00:34:00.382
And you buy those
things a la carte,

00:34:00.382 --> 00:34:02.840
which means you get multiple
bills and multiple contracts--

00:34:02.840 --> 00:34:05.510
because each one of those
data have their own usage

00:34:05.510 --> 00:34:08.449
restrictions, have their
own acceptable use policies,

00:34:08.449 --> 00:34:11.389
have their own prices,
usually their own legal terms,

00:34:11.389 --> 00:34:13.040
that sort of stuff--

00:34:13.040 --> 00:34:16.010
to more of a carnival model.

00:34:16.010 --> 00:34:18.920
So when you go to a-- how many
of you have been to a carnival

00:34:18.920 --> 00:34:20.000
before?

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:20.760
OK.

00:34:20.760 --> 00:34:22.880
When you go to a
carnival, you buy

00:34:22.880 --> 00:34:25.580
a roll of tickets at the door.

00:34:25.580 --> 00:34:26.480
That's your bill.

00:34:26.480 --> 00:34:28.130
And that's your contract.

00:34:28.130 --> 00:34:30.260
And then you go
inside the carnival.

00:34:30.260 --> 00:34:35.131
And you spend your tickets
on different rides, or goods,

00:34:35.131 --> 00:34:35.630
or services.

00:34:38.139 --> 00:34:39.969
And so one ride might
cost 10 tickets.

00:34:39.969 --> 00:34:41.120
Another might cost five.

00:34:41.120 --> 00:34:42.880
Another might cost
three, or two, or one,

00:34:42.880 --> 00:34:44.900
or something like that.

00:34:44.900 --> 00:34:47.560
But we took a tip from
that playbook, and said,

00:34:47.560 --> 00:34:50.050
let's apply that
to the complexity

00:34:50.050 --> 00:34:53.800
that we have around
variable-valued products

00:34:53.800 --> 00:34:55.270
and how we price them.

00:34:55.270 --> 00:35:00.640
So what we thought was, OK, if
we open up a store for APIs,

00:35:00.640 --> 00:35:04.330
when they come in, let's let
them sign that one contract

00:35:04.330 --> 00:35:07.180
that covers all
the APIs, and then

00:35:07.180 --> 00:35:11.590
get their roll of the tickets
that they can then go and spend

00:35:11.590 --> 00:35:14.440
in any way that they would
like on any and all APIs

00:35:14.440 --> 00:35:19.260
that we have at that store.

00:35:19.260 --> 00:35:20.640
So that's what we did.

00:35:20.640 --> 00:35:26.520
In working with Apigee,
we were able to introduce

00:35:26.520 --> 00:35:29.040
one virtual currency,
which we don't call tickets

00:35:29.040 --> 00:35:31.470
from a carnival, we
call them credits.

00:35:31.470 --> 00:35:34.470
And you can buy these
credits, either up front,

00:35:34.470 --> 00:35:37.150
or you can pay as you go.

00:35:37.150 --> 00:35:39.910
So there is both prepaid
and postpaid options.

00:35:39.910 --> 00:35:42.270
There's one unified contract.

00:35:42.270 --> 00:35:44.550
And there's one unified bill.

00:35:44.550 --> 00:35:48.690
And those were the
common two must-haves

00:35:48.690 --> 00:35:53.970
that had the most pain
for both Janet and Dario

00:35:53.970 --> 00:35:57.810
that we went to set out
and go delivery against,

00:35:57.810 --> 00:35:59.520
in terms of our
promise that we wanted

00:35:59.520 --> 00:36:01.560
to give all of our customers.

00:36:05.312 --> 00:36:07.020
When we did this, it
was really important

00:36:07.020 --> 00:36:09.103
for us to do that unified
billing and contracting.

00:36:09.103 --> 00:36:11.910
Because we wanted to do
everything self-service.

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:13.500
Because we'd never
done that before.

00:36:13.500 --> 00:36:16.530
And selling on premise,
over the years,

00:36:16.530 --> 00:36:19.260
we were pretty good
at selling upmarket.

00:36:19.260 --> 00:36:22.080
But once we got into the
mid-market and the down-market,

00:36:22.080 --> 00:36:24.690
where folks wanted to
do things on their own,

00:36:24.690 --> 00:36:27.690
it became difficult. But through
the self-service delivery,

00:36:27.690 --> 00:36:33.480
we essentially came up
with prepackaged plans that

00:36:33.480 --> 00:36:36.600
allowed us to automatically
provision our monitoring

00:36:36.600 --> 00:36:39.170
systems for transactions
that were being used,

00:36:39.170 --> 00:36:42.000
automate billing,
automate contracting, all

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:45.660
of the things that reduce the
friction that made it complex.

00:36:45.660 --> 00:36:49.830
And we removed the comments
like, we love your products,

00:36:49.830 --> 00:36:51.054
but we hate your process.

00:36:51.054 --> 00:36:52.470
So we've really
fixed the process,

00:36:52.470 --> 00:36:55.377
which I call the other 80%.

00:36:55.377 --> 00:36:57.210
And we did it specifically
for self-service.

00:36:57.210 --> 00:37:01.650
Now that said,
what we've found is

00:37:01.650 --> 00:37:03.244
that self-service,
in and of itself,

00:37:03.244 --> 00:37:05.160
requires all of us things
that Bala mentioned,

00:37:05.160 --> 00:37:08.880
in terms of iterating
and capturing metrics

00:37:08.880 --> 00:37:12.960
on how you're engaging,
retention marketing things.

00:37:12.960 --> 00:37:15.791
Sol like once they sign up, and
you collect that sign-up data,

00:37:15.791 --> 00:37:17.040
you're putting that somewhere.

00:37:17.040 --> 00:37:18.690
And then you're monitoring
their transactions.

00:37:18.690 --> 00:37:20.040
Are they active, or
are they inactive?

00:37:20.040 --> 00:37:21.410
If they're inactive,
what's your campaign?

00:37:21.410 --> 00:37:22.660
What's your outreach strategy?

00:37:22.660 --> 00:37:24.837
If they're active, what's
your outreach strategy?

00:37:24.837 --> 00:37:25.920
Who do you assign to that?

00:37:25.920 --> 00:37:27.420
How do you do fulfillment
and follow up?

00:37:27.420 --> 00:37:28.950
How do you get them to
upgrade or downgrade?

00:37:28.950 --> 00:37:30.491
Do you let them swap
out their cards,

00:37:30.491 --> 00:37:34.460
and all those kinds of things
that are in the other 80%

00:37:34.460 --> 00:37:37.655
that we have to think
about as product people?

00:37:37.655 --> 00:37:39.780
Or else we're going to end
up on the phone handling

00:37:39.780 --> 00:37:42.030
all the customer support
escalations, which none of us

00:37:42.030 --> 00:37:43.374
want to do.

00:37:43.374 --> 00:37:44.790
So we get it right
the first time.

00:37:44.790 --> 00:37:49.320
But that said, that was my plan.

00:37:49.320 --> 00:37:52.050
And I thought that
self-service would work.

00:37:52.050 --> 00:37:56.730
And what we found is that we
had a gap in several areas

00:37:56.730 --> 00:37:58.380
of the business
where we still know

00:37:58.380 --> 00:38:02.290
we need to improve,
particularly around retention,

00:38:02.290 --> 00:38:07.620
and communicating, and
opening up a two-way dialect

00:38:07.620 --> 00:38:10.294
and channel that's digital.

00:38:10.294 --> 00:38:12.210
We're still going to
continue to work on that.

00:38:12.210 --> 00:38:13.626
But as a result
of what we learned

00:38:13.626 --> 00:38:18.180
from the self-service
channel that we opened,

00:38:18.180 --> 00:38:20.997
we've decided to
go back to using

00:38:20.997 --> 00:38:23.580
our direct sellers, who've have
been selling on premise for so

00:38:23.580 --> 00:38:27.790
long, to come and now sell these
self-service things, as well.

00:38:27.790 --> 00:38:30.570
So I guess the
point of that being,

00:38:30.570 --> 00:38:34.140
or that point of that story,
is that we all, sometimes,

00:38:34.140 --> 00:38:39.000
have a vision at the origin,
or at the beginning of where

00:38:39.000 --> 00:38:40.110
we start.

00:38:40.110 --> 00:38:41.810
And the journey
takes us in a variety

00:38:41.810 --> 00:38:42.810
of different directions.

00:38:42.810 --> 00:38:43.518
And we persevere.

00:38:43.518 --> 00:38:44.920
And we pivot.

00:38:44.920 --> 00:38:47.220
We've recently gone
through a pivot change.

00:38:47.220 --> 00:38:54.270
But all of this we couldn't
have done without Google's help.

00:38:54.270 --> 00:38:59.550
And I mentioned that point
around the direct sale stuff,

00:38:59.550 --> 00:39:01.980
because it's going to
introduce a whole other set

00:39:01.980 --> 00:39:05.400
of complications that are
different from the way

00:39:05.400 --> 00:39:06.580
that we set up self-service.

00:39:06.580 --> 00:39:09.300
And they're going to be
things like, how do you

00:39:09.300 --> 00:39:14.250
set up custom plans, and
then provision those,

00:39:14.250 --> 00:39:19.680
and create custom bills,
and things like that?

00:39:19.680 --> 00:39:22.350
So there was another
session track,

00:39:22.350 --> 00:39:24.150
I think earlier today,
about how that's

00:39:24.150 --> 00:39:27.530
going to be done going forward.

00:39:27.530 --> 00:39:32.100
And Google's made another
acquisition in that area.

00:39:32.100 --> 00:39:34.629
So we're pretty
stoked about that too.

00:39:34.629 --> 00:39:36.420
Because we know that
that's an entirely set

00:39:36.420 --> 00:39:40.140
of new complications that
are right in front of us.

00:39:40.140 --> 00:39:43.650
In fact, we're seeing that now.

00:39:43.650 --> 00:39:44.470
Thank you.

00:39:44.470 --> 00:39:48.096
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:48.096 --> 00:39:49.470
BALA KASIVISWANATHAN:
So I always

00:39:49.470 --> 00:39:50.844
find it fascinating
when you have

00:39:50.844 --> 00:39:53.470
customers-- let's talk about
this for a couple of reasons.

00:39:53.470 --> 00:39:59.700
Number one, especially being
in the Bay Area and Silicon

00:39:59.700 --> 00:40:04.560
Valley, you get used to fairly
cutting-edge technology,

00:40:04.560 --> 00:40:08.310
cutting-edge businesses, which
are built like over the last 10

00:40:08.310 --> 00:40:09.930
years, or five years.

00:40:09.930 --> 00:40:12.390
And the speed at which
you can innovate and do

00:40:12.390 --> 00:40:15.270
interesting things
is very different.

00:40:15.270 --> 00:40:20.000
And then to see a company
like Pitney Bowes built

00:40:20.000 --> 00:40:22.257
a business over last 100
years, I guess, right?

00:40:22.257 --> 00:40:23.340
JON SPINNEY: That's right.

00:40:23.340 --> 00:40:24.881
BALA KASIVISWANATHAN:
And then trying

00:40:24.881 --> 00:40:30.300
to do the shift with all the
importance of the existing

00:40:30.300 --> 00:40:32.790
business, while trying
to shift and create

00:40:32.790 --> 00:40:34.660
a new way to do the business.

00:40:34.660 --> 00:40:36.304
And that's the challenge.

00:40:36.304 --> 00:40:37.470
That's really the challenge.

00:40:37.470 --> 00:40:40.740
Because if all of us product
managers had a clean sheet,

00:40:40.740 --> 00:40:42.510
and we could go
build a product, it's

00:40:42.510 --> 00:40:44.160
become so much more easier.

00:40:44.160 --> 00:40:46.200
But all of us live
in the real world

00:40:46.200 --> 00:40:49.050
where we have to deal
with constraints,

00:40:49.050 --> 00:40:51.529
which are our existing
business constraints.

00:40:51.529 --> 00:40:53.820
And sometimes, what happens
is the constraint is money.

00:40:53.820 --> 00:40:55.440
Like you can't just go
to town, because you

00:40:55.440 --> 00:40:57.690
don't have the money to
go market raise awareness.

00:40:57.690 --> 00:41:02.460
In turn, sometimes, we
don't have the right product

00:41:02.460 --> 00:41:04.500
features.

00:41:04.500 --> 00:41:07.680
And one thing, none of
us have enough engineers

00:41:07.680 --> 00:41:09.660
in our engineering
teams ever, right?

00:41:09.660 --> 00:41:12.100
So all these constraints
happen to all of us.

00:41:12.100 --> 00:41:15.150
So the question is, how do we
do the right thing, in terms

00:41:15.150 --> 00:41:17.250
of opening up, and
driving the business,

00:41:17.250 --> 00:41:20.020
and then innovating
as we go forward?

00:41:20.020 --> 00:41:25.530
So hopefully you've got some
real, unfiltered feedback here

00:41:25.530 --> 00:41:27.630
from Jon.

00:41:27.630 --> 00:41:30.570
And I kind of requested him
not to paint, necessarily,

00:41:30.570 --> 00:41:34.080
a rosy picture, nor a very dire
picture, but a real picture,

00:41:34.080 --> 00:41:36.780
so all of you can take
away the right things,

00:41:36.780 --> 00:41:39.190
and think about how it
impacts your business.

00:41:39.190 --> 00:41:42.990
I almost felt, Jon, that
your talk could land up

00:41:42.990 --> 00:41:47.730
in the classic meme, where
you have what I think I do,

00:41:47.730 --> 00:41:49.920
versus what my mom
thinks I do, and what

00:41:49.920 --> 00:41:51.210
really happens kind of thing.

00:41:51.210 --> 00:41:54.360
I think you could do
some version of that.

00:41:54.360 --> 00:41:56.430
But to wrap it up
here, so what are

00:41:56.430 --> 00:41:59.279
we going to do just
kind of reinforce what

00:41:59.279 --> 00:42:01.320
we've been telling, I
think, through the session.

00:42:01.320 --> 00:42:04.500
Number one is, if you're
an API product manager,

00:42:04.500 --> 00:42:06.900
and you are thinking about
API product management,

00:42:06.900 --> 00:42:09.960
absolutely focus on
your developer personas.

00:42:09.960 --> 00:42:11.760
And I'm calling them
developer personas,

00:42:11.760 --> 00:42:14.320
because Jon correctly
pointed out,

00:42:14.320 --> 00:42:16.110
sometimes, these are
product manager kind

00:42:16.110 --> 00:42:17.550
of people on the other side.

00:42:17.550 --> 00:42:19.890
Sometimes, these are
actual developers.

00:42:19.890 --> 00:42:23.640
It depends on your business,
and what kind of APIs you have.

00:42:23.640 --> 00:42:26.370
The second one, I think,
which is loud and clear,

00:42:26.370 --> 00:42:30.060
and I'll say it once again,
is please make things

00:42:30.060 --> 00:42:32.550
easy to access and discover.

00:42:32.550 --> 00:42:37.740
I cannot insist on this more.

00:42:37.740 --> 00:42:40.050
Because the one thing
I think everybody

00:42:40.050 --> 00:42:44.970
is getting used to is going,
and searching, or discovering

00:42:44.970 --> 00:42:46.590
things themselves
before they actually

00:42:46.590 --> 00:42:48.250
want to talk to somebody.

00:42:48.250 --> 00:42:51.030
And if you're not
surfacing in the way they

00:42:51.030 --> 00:42:53.580
want to consume your
information upfront,

00:42:53.580 --> 00:42:55.420
you lose them way before.

00:42:55.420 --> 00:42:59.020
So please, please make sure
that it's available very easily.

00:42:59.020 --> 00:43:01.270
And the reason you have
a developer product

00:43:01.270 --> 00:43:04.530
PitneyBowes.com, or any
of the other sites--

00:43:04.530 --> 00:43:05.670
and make sure it's indexed.

00:43:05.670 --> 00:43:07.170
Make sure it's
available on search--

00:43:07.170 --> 00:43:10.140
is that you are
going to find people

00:43:10.140 --> 00:43:12.220
doing exactly what you do.

00:43:12.220 --> 00:43:14.880
When you're going to buy
a gift for your partner,

00:43:14.880 --> 00:43:18.240
or spouse, or your
kid, you go and search.

00:43:18.240 --> 00:43:20.127
And everybody does that.

00:43:20.127 --> 00:43:22.710
And you've got to make sure that
your product is discoverable,

00:43:22.710 --> 00:43:25.110
and then from there,
it's easily accessible.

00:43:25.110 --> 00:43:26.940
And APIs are no different.

00:43:26.940 --> 00:43:29.790
The last one is, please
remember to deliver

00:43:29.790 --> 00:43:32.310
a consistent experience.

00:43:32.310 --> 00:43:35.250
And when I say
consistent, I'm not

00:43:35.250 --> 00:43:39.120
suggesting that you deliver
a white glove experience.

00:43:39.120 --> 00:43:41.170
There's a difference.

00:43:41.170 --> 00:43:44.070
There are products for which you
need a white glove experience.

00:43:44.070 --> 00:43:47.370
But consistency means, every
time I interact with you,

00:43:47.370 --> 00:43:49.680
I know what I'm
going to get back.

00:43:49.680 --> 00:43:54.390
So as long as that is
there, I'm OK with it.

00:43:54.390 --> 00:43:57.990
But if, like Jon was saying, if
I go for location intelligence

00:43:57.990 --> 00:44:00.990
API, and I get one experience,
and I go to something else,

00:44:00.990 --> 00:44:03.219
and I get a different
experience, I'm going,

00:44:03.219 --> 00:44:04.260
this is the same company?

00:44:04.260 --> 00:44:04.760
Come on.

00:44:04.760 --> 00:44:05.910
What's happening here?

00:44:05.910 --> 00:44:09.780
So it is not about the
high touch or low touch

00:44:09.780 --> 00:44:10.680
aspect of it.

00:44:10.680 --> 00:44:13.560
It's about the consistency
of the experience you want

00:44:13.560 --> 00:44:15.030
to deliver to your developers.

00:44:15.030 --> 00:44:16.920
That's the most important thing.

00:44:16.920 --> 00:44:18.360
That brings people back.

00:44:18.360 --> 00:44:21.330
And they know what to
expect, they are productive.

00:44:21.330 --> 00:44:22.650
You can then improve.

00:44:22.650 --> 00:44:24.300
And every time you
improve, they can

00:44:24.300 --> 00:44:26.610
see that you're making the
change, which is consistent,

00:44:26.610 --> 00:44:27.430
as well.

00:44:27.430 --> 00:44:27.930
OK?

00:44:27.930 --> 00:44:29.370
Thank you again for your time.

00:44:29.370 --> 00:44:32.440
And Jon and I will be around
for any other the questions.

00:44:32.440 --> 00:44:32.940
Thank you.

00:44:32.940 --> 00:44:34.740
JON SPINNEY: Thank you.

00:44:34.740 --> 00:44:36.890
